{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEMX5ujeNGrpKZXAYKve","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed three separate bugs, and each one blocked connecting by itself. The full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`. I tested the whole flow against a local fake of Intervals; I couldn't test against the real intervals.icu because your rules rule out calls to outside services.\n\n1. **The Connect button always got \"unauthorized\".** The button was a plain link. Your sign-in token only travels in a header that the app's own request code adds, and a browser never adds that header when you just follow a link. So the server saw no sign-in and refused every athlete. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals sign-in link using the app's normal request code, then sends the browser there. The change is in `server.py` and `static/app.js`.\n\n2. **Intervals rejected the request before showing the consent screen.** The calendar sync released on 2026-10-02 changed the requested permissions to ask for both read and write access to activities and to the calendar. Intervals takes one entry per area, and write already includes read; Rivera's token from before that change shows this format. I changed the permissions in `config.py` to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which still gives the same access.\n   - This cause is worked out from the logs; I couldn't confirm it. If the error continues, check whether the app you registered with Intervals is allowed calendar access and the exact callback address.\n\n3. **Rivera's connection \"expired\" after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire, and Intervals sends no expiry time and no refresh token. The code assumed a one-hour lifetime when none was given, then threw away a token that was still valid. Now a connection is only marked expired if Intervals actually gives an expiry, or if Intervals rejects the token (meaning the athlete revoked access).\n\nI also made two small changes:\n- If an athlete clicks Deny on the Intervals consent screen, they now go back to the app instead of seeing an error page.\n- The log line for a new connection now records the access Intervals granted.\n\nRivera will need to connect once more, because the old token was already cleared.\n\nSomething else is already running on port 8766, so I ran my test copy on port 8777 and didn't touch that process. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1897,"cache_read_input_tokens":36974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1897},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4992698-9775-4b10-ba34-6f1e71f634e7","uuid":"e3e91c33-a9a7-471e-9099-d888989adceb","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:26:50.905Z","request_id":"req_011CgEMX5Wfdey9zd18a1PNs"}
{"duration_api_ms":93910,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a4992698-9775-4b10-ba34-6f1e71f634e7","total_cost_usd":0.40784639999999994,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22512,"cache_read_input_tokens":224732,"output_tokens":9137,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2004},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22512,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":753,"cache_read_input_tokens":36974,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1897},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":9137,"cacheReadInputTokens":224732,"cacheCreationInputTokens":22512,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.40784639999999994,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2004,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":8,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I fixed three separate bugs, and each one blocked connecting by itself. The full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`. I tested the whole flow against a local fake of Intervals; I couldn't test against the real intervals.icu because your rules rule out calls to outside services.\n\n1. **The Connect button always got \"unauthorized\".** The button was a plain link. Your sign-in token only travels in a header that the app's own request code adds, and a browser never adds that header when you just follow a link. So the server saw no sign-in and refused every athlete. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals sign-in link using the app's normal request code, then sends the browser there. The change is in `server.py` and `static/app.js`.\n\n2. **Intervals rejected the request before showing the consent screen.** The calendar sync released on 2026-10-02 changed the requested permissions to ask for both read and write access to activities and to the calendar. Intervals takes one entry per area, and write already includes read; Rivera's token from before that change shows this format. I changed the permissions in `config.py` to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which still gives the same access.\n   - This cause is worked out from the logs; I couldn't confirm it. If the error continues, check whether the app you registered with Intervals is allowed calendar access and the exact callback address.\n\n3. **Rivera's connection \"expired\" after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire, and Intervals sends no expiry time and no refresh token. The code assumed a one-hour lifetime when none was given, then threw away a token that was still valid. Now a connection is only marked expired if Intervals actually gives an expiry, or if Intervals rejects the token (meaning the athlete revoked access).\n\nI also made two small changes:\n- If an athlete clicks Deny on the Intervals consent screen, they now go back to the app instead of seeing an error page.\n- The log line for a new connection now records the access Intervals granted.\n\nRivera will need to connect once more, because the old token was already cleared.\n\nSomething else is already running on port 8766, so I ran my test copy on port 8777 and didn't touch that process. Nothing is committed.","ttft_ms":2587,"type":"result","duration_ms":98078,"uuid":"51f170fd-3283-4fdd-89ad-d28e04cd9